    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Maple Leafs spent the spring smashing into the same wall - too little finish, too much leakage, and not enough answers when the pressure tightened. <strong>The season ended at 32-36-14, eighth in the Atlantic Division, with 78 points and a 15th-place playoff seed</strong>, a blunt verdict on a club that never found its footing and finished on a seven-game skid. That record tells the story plainly enough: 31 games behind and out of the picture, with the rebuild-or-retool argument now moving from coffee-shop grumbling to the front office. Matthew Knies has been under scrutiny after an inconsistent campaign, and that only sharpens the offseason question around the young core - who is part of the next Leafs team, who gets paid, and who gets pushed out? The club's first task is not chasing ghosts from this winter; it is deciding whether the coaching staff remains intact, how aggressively to attack free agency, and whether the draft needs to be a lifeline or just another footnote. <strong>For Toronto, the real season starts now - with hard choices, and no hiding place</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 29, 2026"
      },
