    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "28-29",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Momentum has finally stopped slipping through the Blue Jays' fingers - and the standings are noticing. <strong>Wednesday night's 2-1 squeeze job over Baltimore gave Toronto a third straight win</strong>, and it left the club at 28-29, third in the AL East and sitting in the sixth playoff spot, a half-game under water but still very much alive in the chase. That is the story now: not domination, but survival with a little bite. The Jays have won 10 of their last 14, which is the kind of run that drags a team back into the conversation one hard-nosed inning at a time. Yohendrick Piñango's bases-loaded walk in the eighth was the winning stroke, and Vladimir Guerrero Jr. got the job done at first on the final out - the sort of clean, unfussy baseball that travels. There is still ground to make up, with eight games separating them from the top line, but this club has steadied itself after the Marlins wobble. <strong>Next comes another look at Baltimore on Friday at 6:05 p.m. ET, and Toronto has a chance to turn a useful week into a statement.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 29, 2026"
      },
