      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The draft has just finished, and the Commanders are in the familiar business of selling hope while the roster still has some holes in plain sight. Washington sits <strong>3rd in the NFC East</strong>, <strong>6 games back</strong>, with a <strong>W1</strong> streak and a <strong>14th-place playoff seed</strong> in the early bookkeeping, but the real story is the rookie class - and <strong>Round 3, No. 71 overall, Carnell Tate arrived as the first big swing, a downfield target built to stretch the field and give Jayden Daniels a cleaner throwing menu</strong>. That fits a team that has been trying to upgrade its secondary and sharpen its passing game at the same time; the front office cannot afford another draft that looks pretty on paper and soft in the trenches. Tate is the name to watch because he can change the geometry of the offense fast if he wins early snaps, while the rest of the class and the undrafted signings will be judged on whether they can add depth where Washington has been thin. <strong>What matters next is simple: which rookies can get on the field by camp and which ones are just camp bodies with fancy college résumés.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 29, 2026"
      },
